From d1c7c1dba2939d7fc61bc38d493c6f66bb7095a5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Artur Szafraniec <ganggg111@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 21 Sep 2026 12:51:23 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 14/40] serial: s5p: leave the divider alone when the rate
 makes no sense

The register holds one less than the number of clocks in a bit, so a rate
that gives fewer than sixteen of them is written as a borrow, that is, as
the largest divider there is.  The port never drains again, and because the
uclass retries a full port for as long as it stays full, the first
character of the banner never returns.  On a phone the hang detector left
armed by the vendor's first stage turns that into a reset, which looks like
a boot loop with no output at all.

Whatever ran before us had the port working, so the safe answer is to keep
its divider rather than write a nonsense one.

This is not hypothetical.  A clock fix that made a rate correct rather than
wrong is what produced such a rate, and the boot loop that followed was
read for a long time as a fault in the clock tree.

Signed-off-by: Artur Szafraniec <ganggg111@gmail.com>
---
 drivers/serial/serial_s5p.c | 11 +++++++++++
 1 file changed, 11 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/serial/serial_s5p.c b/drivers/serial/serial_s5p.c
index d46a8861..8391224c 100644
--- a/drivers/serial/serial_s5p.c
+++ b/drivers/serial/serial_s5p.c
@@ -107,6 +107,17 @@ static void __maybe_unused s5p_serial_baud(struct s5p_uart *uart, u8 reg_width,
 
 	val = uclk / baudrate;
 
+	/*
+	 * The register holds one less than the number of clocks in a bit, so
+	 * fewer than sixteen of them would be written as a borrow, that is, as
+	 * the largest divider there is.  The port would never drain again, and
+	 * because the uclass retries a full port for as long as it stays full,
+	 * the first character of the banner would never return.  Leave the
+	 * divider alone instead: whatever ran before us had the port working.
+	 */
+	if (val < 16)
+		return;
+
 	writel(val / 16 - 1, &uart->ubrdiv);
 
 	if (s5p_uart_divslot())
